# The variant registry. Every script on `main` reads this and nothing else to decide what
# "all variants" means, so adding or retiring a variant is a one-line edit here.
#
#   <variant>  <branch>  <family>  <capabilities>
#
# `capabilities` is a comma-separated list, or `-` for none. It records workload knobs the
# branch actually implements SERVER-side, which is not the same as knobs k6 will send: k6 is
# byte-identical everywhere and sends them all, and Spring ignores unknown properties, so an
# unimplemented knob is silently discarded rather than rejected. run-suite.sh reads this to
# warn when a knob is set for variants that cannot honour it.
#
#   reserve-delay   RESERVE_DELAY_MS — `reserveDelayMs` on CreateItemRequest plus a sleep on
#                   the reserve path, paid only once the reserve is known to succeed and with
#                   the row/aggregate lock held. Every branch here, since 2026-08-06. TO
#                   needs a column (V5__reserve_delay.sql on TO-1/TO-2/TO-4; V2 on TO-3 and
#                   on TO-3-pessimistic, which forked from it); ES
#                   needs none — the value rides InventoryCreatedEvent and is replayed into
#                   the aggregate, with the sleep in the @CommandHandler so a replay or
#                   snapshot load does not pay it.
#
#   payload-bytes   PAYLOAD_BYTES as a real per-reserve cost. Every branch here, since
#                   2026-08-06. ES needs no schema change — the padding rides
#                   InventoryCreatedEvent and is replayed into the aggregate. On TO it has to
#                   be a column on inventory_state, which only TO-3 had; TO-1/TO-2/TO-4
#                   gained it as V6__additional_bytes.sql. Before that the field was accepted
#                   and carried on the creation event, but never reached the row each reserve
#                   rewrites, so the copy-on-write cost the lever measures never happened.
#
# `variant` is the identity used everywhere downstream: it names the image tag
# (inventory-reservation-<variant>:latest, lowercased), the worktree directory, and the
# bench-results/<variant>_<scenario>_<ts>/ run directory. There is no bench.env any more:
# run-suite.sh exports VARIANT from this column and bench.sh builds RUN_NAME from it, so the
# name here is the only name a run is ever filed under. It need not equal the branch name —
# the two columns are separate for exactly that reason — but keeping them equal is what makes
# `--only <name>` guessable.
#
# THE LIVE SET IS NINE, as of 2026-08-23: TO-1..TO-4, TO-2-fix-A, TO-2-push, and ES-1, ES-2, ES-4.
# That is what the campaign runs and what every cross-variant table is built from.
#
# THREE of those nine are arms off TO-2, which is why the TO column is wider than the family
# deserves at a glance:
#
#   TO-2         the drain reads `seq > cursor`                     (V8)
#   TO-2-fix-A   the drain reads a transaction-id watermark         (V9)  — same trigger as TO-2
#   TO-2-push    the NOTIFY carries the event; there is no drain    (V10) — same schema as TO-2
#
# Each is single-variable AGAINST TO-2, not against each other: fix-A and push change different
# things and share no code. Read the column as three separate two-arm comparisons.
#
# The set was seven from 2026-08-20, eight when TO-2-push landed, nine when TO-2-fix-A was
# registered — all on 2026-08-23. Any runbook or table citing "the seven" or "the eight" predates
# this and its phase-1 counts are short.
#
# RETIRED VARIANTS ARE DOCUMENTED IN docs/retired-variants.md, not here — TO-2-opt and ES-3 (both
# cut on 2026-08-20), TO-3-pessimistic, TO-2-newT/TO-3-newT, and the ES-4-NullLock-A/-mod/-retry
# sidecars that were never rows. Each entry there says why it went and what re-adding it needs.
# Read it BEFORE re-adding a row or re-running an archived pair: most of those pairs stopped being
# single-variable before they were pulled, which is usually why they were pulled.
#
# `TO-2-fix-A` IS TO-2 WITH A DIFFERENT STOPPING RULE (added to the registry 2026-08-23; branch
# built 2026-08-23 off TO-2 @ 6f6c7bc). The trigger, the payload and the delivery shape are TO-2's
# exactly. What changes is where the drain stops.
#
# TO-2's cursor advances to the highest `seq` it has SEEN, and a publication's seq is assigned at
# INSERT — statement 1 of OrderWriteCommandHandler.write — while the inventory_state row locks are
# statement 4. So a transaction holds a low seq and commits after higher ones were already drained:
# sequence order is not commit order, and the cursor routinely moves PAST rows that had not
# committed, stranding them where only the sweep can reach. Measured on a 400-order, 40-way burst
# over 4 shared rows: 101 of 1458 publications (6.9%) stranded, undelivered for ~100 s.
#
# V9 makes the cursor a boundary the drain can PROVE instead of one it guesses: `xact_id xid8
# DEFAULT pg_current_xact_id()`, a partial index on (xact_id, seq), and a SEPARATE
# outbox_cursor.xact_position column (the two arms must not share state when the knob flips on one
# database). Each pass reads `W = pg_snapshot_xmin(pg_current_snapshot())` ONCE and delivers the
# half-open window [cursor, W). Every transaction with xid < W was already decided when W was read,
# so nothing can appear behind the drain. Same burst: 0 of 1564 stranded.
#
# `app.outbox-cursor.watermark` defaults TRUE here — the branch IS the arm. V9's column and index
# made two images unavoidable anyway, so it is deliberately NOT routed through docker-compose and
# main needs no passthrough; OUTBOX_CURSOR_WATERMARK=false still flips it locally. The startup line
# logs `[OUTBOX] drain mode=WATERMARK`, so a run cannot record the wrong arm.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT WILL MISLEAD YOU WHEN COMPARING IT TO TO-2:
#
# 1. `outbox_cursor_position` and `outbox_cursor_lag` CHANGE UNIT with the arm. TO-2 counts sequence
#    entries; this counts transaction ids. The panels resolve on both and the numbers are not
#    comparable — OutboxMetrics is arm-aware precisely because it once read outbox_cursor.position,
#    which this arm never writes, and the lag gauge grew with the whole sequence and read as a
#    totally stalled drain. A bench run cannot catch that, because "stalled drain" looks plausible.
# 2. HEAD-OF-LINE BLOCKING is the price. One slow writer pins xmin and ALL delivery waits behind it,
#    which is the opposite failure mode to TO-2's stranding. Watch
#    pg_stat_activity_max_tx_duration; it hit 12 s during the 2026-08-15 collapse. The sweep here is
#    therefore the position-free republishByScan: nothing is stranded BELOW the cursor, but a pinned
#    xmin strands rows ABOVE it, which no cursor-bounded query reaches.
#
# EXPECT IT TO STILL FAIL CAPACITY. Delivery was never the whole problem: the same burst rejected
# 315/400 orders with `Optimistic lock failed` and 1790 retries (4.5/order). Bounding reserve
# concurrency is a separate change, to be measured on its own.
#
# `TO-2-push` IS TO-2 WITH ONE THING CHANGED (added 2026-08-23, off TO-2 @ 6f6c7bc): what a NOTIFY
# carries. TO-2 sends the publication id and the consumer reads the row back; TO-2-push sends the
# event itself — id, event type, listener id, serialized event — and the consumer claims and
# delivers without ever finding the row. Two statements per delivery become one, and the outbox's
# read path leaves the hot path entirely.
#
# WHY THE PAIR IS SINGLE-VARIABLE, AND WHAT IT COST TO KEEP IT THAT WAY. V8's `seq` column, its
# partial index and the `outbox_cursor` table are all still there on TO-2-push, unused by delivery.
# They exist only to make the read path cheap, so the honest version of this branch would drop them
# — that was considered and rejected, because dropping them changes the write path too (one fewer
# index entry per insert, one fewer cleanup per completion) and the column would then vary two
# things at once. Read the pair as "how the event reaches the consumer", not as "what push costs at
# its best". A follow-up branch that drops V8 would measure the latter.
#
# WHAT TO WATCH, and it is not the knee. The interesting series are:
#   outbox_notify_received_total   deliveries that took the push path
#   outbox_sweep_rescued_total     deliveries that did not — see the oversize note below
#   outbox_notify_queue_depth      deliveries queued ahead of the pool (client-side backpressure)
#   outbox_notify_queue_usage      pg_notification_queue_usage(), 0..1 — the SERVER side, and the
#                                  only series that shows backpressure reaching the writers
# `outbox_cursor_position` and `outbox_cursor_lag` are NOT published on this branch. The cursor no
# longer advances, so a lag computed against the sequence high-water mark would climb forever and
# read as a totally stalled drain. Neither is on a dashboard, so nothing goes blank; a panel added
# for them later would read empty here, correctly.
#
# THE OPEN LOOP IS THE RISK, and it is bounded rather than avoided. A payload-carrying notification
# cannot be coalesced — each carries different bytes — so this branch submits one delivery per
# NOTIFY, which is the shape commit 2185068 removed from TO-2 after it drove db_write p95 to 404 ms
# and conflict_ratio to 4.64. What makes it safe here is that the delivery pool's queue is bounded
# and a full queue BLOCKS the listener rather than rejecting: PostgreSQL's async-notify queue then
# backs up and writers slow at pg_notify. EVENT_DELIVERY_QUEUE_CAPACITY (compose, bare form,
# default 1000) is where that limit sits. If this branch collapses under load the way the 2026-08-15
# TO-2 run did, that knob and outbox_notify_queue_usage are where to look first — but expect the
# failure to look like slowed ADMISSION, not a growing backlog, which is the opposite signature.
#
# ONE EXPECTED ARTEFACT, not a regression. pg_notify raises above 8000 bytes, and NOTIFY is queued
# at commit, so sending an oversize message would fail an already-committed business transaction.
# The V10 trigger therefore stays SILENT above ~7900 bytes and the sweep delivers those rows a sweep
# interval later. PAYLOAD_BYTES reaches the server only through POST /inventory (k6/lib/api.js), so
# at the C10/C11 cells every seeded InventoryCreatedEvent lands there: expect
# publish_lag{eventType="InventoryCreatedEvent"} to carry a ~60 s tail on this branch and not on
# TO-2, and outbox_sweep_rescued_total to equal DISTINCT_ITEMS rather than 0. The order path is
# never affected — it sends no additionalBytesSize — so `payload-bytes` stays in the capability
# column: the knob IS honoured, on inventory_state.additional_bytes, rewritten by every reserve.
#
# `TO-3` ABSORBED THE FORMER TO-3-mod AND TO-3-mod-A (merged 2026-08-17, PR #4). Those were
# separate registry rows until then; they are gone, and TO-3 now carries both changes. Any run
# directory named `TO-3` from BEFORE that merge measured the old branch — different retry mechanism,
# one flat pool, 200/200 threads — so do not put pre- and post-merge TO-3 rows in one table.
#
# Two things came in with it, on top of the outbox design TO-3 always had.
#
# 1. THE CONFLICT RETRY MECHANISM, rebuilt to match the ES branches'. Retry POLICY is unchanged
#    (5 attempts, 25/50/100/200 ms, same counters), so nothing measured can be blamed on a different
#    attempt budget or backoff curve. The old path waited inside Spring's `@Retryable` interceptor,
#    which sleeps on the `order-worker` thread that is retrying — up to 375 ms of a worker doing
#    nothing, and under load the pool could sit entirely parked in backoff while orders queued
#    behind it. It now schedules instead of sleeping and runs the retried attempt on a pool of its
#    own, which is how ES has always worked (Axon's `RetryingCallback` re-dispatches inline onto its
#    retry pool). Both properties used to be confounds bundled into every TO-vs-ES number.
#
#    Which topology a run uses is a setting, and a run must record it:
#      ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL=true   (default) retries run on their own pool, ES-shaped.
#      ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL=false  retries hand back to the worker pool.
#    They are not merely different widths: on the hand-off path a retry rejoins a FIFO queue at the
#    TAIL and waits behind orders admitted after it, so its real wait is backoff + queue depth.
#
#    Expect a HIGHER exhaustion rate than the pre-merge branch in either topology — work that no
#    longer blocks admits more concurrent attempts, hence more conflicts.
#
# 2. THE LANE WIDTHS: worker 200 -> 150, retry 12 -> 50, http 200 -> 99.
#
#    These arrived with a lane-routed TWO-POOL DataSource (app-pool for HTTP reads, write-pool for
#    everything else), which was REMOVED again on 2026-08-17. The widths stayed; the split did not.
#    TO is back to one flat Hikari pool on every branch, sized by DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS in .env (350).
#    The split lived on this branch from 2026-08-15 to 2026-08-17 — git history has it if the
#    read-isolation argument is ever wanted back. Nothing reads DB_APP_POOL_SIZE or
#    DB_WRITE_POOL_SIZE any more and compose no longer forwards them.
#
# It publishes four series the pre-rebuild branches do not — `order_retry_backoff_time` (accumulated
# wait per retried order), `order_retry_rejected_total`, and `order_retry_pool_queued`. Every
# registered TO variant publishes them; the retired TO-2-opt and TO-3-pessimistic still carry the
# blocking `@Retryable` and publish none, so a panel using them reads empty on an archived run of
# either (docs/retired-variants.md).
#
# 3. THE RETRY POOL IS GONE AGAIN, ON ALL FOUR (2026-08-18). TO-3-oneExec proved the topology and
#    TO-1, TO-2, TO-3 and TO-4 all took it, so `app.order-retry` no longer exists on any of them.
#
#    One ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor of 200 threads, all named `order-worker-*`, runs first attempts
#    AND retried attempts: the worker thread that failed the attempt schedules the retry on its own
#    pool and returns, the backoff is served in that pool's DelayedWorkQueue (no thread, no
#    connection), and when it comes due an `order-worker` thread runs it.
#
#    200 = the old 150 worker + 50 retry, so executing width and connection demand are unchanged and
#    the change is retry TOPOLOGY only. NOTHING IN-TREE STILL RUNS THE TWO-POOL TOPOLOGY, so the A/B
#    is against runs taken before 2026-08-18, not against a sibling variant. `TO-3-oneExec` was that
#    sibling and is RETIRED from this registry — it is now the same code as TO-3.
#
#    EVERY ONE OF THE FOUR MUST BE RUN AT THE SAME ORDER_WORKER_THREADS, and every one of them is:
#    docker-compose defaults the variable for every variant, so an unset run gets the same width
#    everywhere. Each branch also logs its width at startup.
#
#    THAT WIDTH IS 50 AS OF 2026-08-25, down from 200. It is a deliberate move into the band this
#    file already predicted the retry A/B would diverge in (see ORDER_WORKER_THREADS below: "expected
#    to show little at 200 and to diverge as it falls (64, 32, 16)"). The consequence is that NO RUN
#    ARCHIVED BEFORE THIS DATE IS COMPARABLE ON THROUGHPUT — every one of them was taken at 200, and
#    each branch's own application.yaml still says 200, which compose overrides. Compare 50-thread
#    runs against 50-thread runs.
#
#    THIS USED TO BE A HAZARD AND IS NOT ANY MORE, so do not re-derive the old workaround. Between
#    2026-08-18 01:29 and 10:21 the tree carried `variants.d/TO-{1,2,3,4}.env`, sourced per variant
#    by run-suite.sh, each exporting the same 200 — written against a claim that compose defaulted
#    the variable to 150. No committed state of this repo ever did: across every ref,
#    `git log --all -S ORDER_WORKER_THREADS -- docker-compose.yml` returns exactly one commit,
#    fd5dff8, which ADDED the variable at 200, and no reachable tree holds a 150. (A 150 living only
#    in an uncommitted working tree on 2026-08-17 cannot be ruled out from git, so a run recorded
#    that day is the one case worth checking against its own env capture.) The files were no-ops for
#    their whole committed life and were deleted on 2026-08-18 with run-suite.sh's sourcing hook.
#
#    200 for the whole TO family is a decision, not a leftover: the retired TO-2-opt and
#    TO-3-pessimistic run there too (docs/retired-variants.md, "Not ported"). The one branch
#    compose's default actually overrides is TO-3-mod-A, whose application.yaml still says 150 —
#    it is not in this registry, so nothing in a suite run depends on that, but a manual run of it
#    does not get the 150 its yaml asks for.
#
#    Do not read the merge as a third retry setting alongside ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL. The
#    queue POSITION a retry lands in is the same as the =false hand-off — a DelayedWorkQueue orders
#    by due time and a first attempt is a zero-delay task, so a retry sorts behind everything
#    submitted before it came due and ahead of everything after, exactly where a FIFO tail puts it.
#    What went away is the second POOL and the cross-pool hand-off, so the result is about pool count
#    and lane isolation, not about queue discipline. Both ORDER_RETRY_* knobs are inert on all four
#    and they say so in their startup logs.
#
#    METRICS CHANGED WITH IT. `order_retry_pool_active` is GONE (no separate pool can be active; a
#    panel target for it now returns nothing on TO-1..TO-4, which is correct rather than broken).
#    `order_retry_pool_queued` IS still published but means something narrower — retries scheduled
#    and not yet started — because the merged pool's `executor_queued_tasks` holds ready first
#    attempts and backing-off retries together. Ready backlog is the difference of the two.
#    `executor_*{name="orderWorkerExecutor"}` is bound by hand, since the pool is not a
#    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor and Boot only auto-binds those. Two of those series read differently
#    now, both because a ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor is not a ThreadPoolTaskExecutor:
#    `executor_pool_max_threads` is 2147483647 and NOT the width (an STPE grows only to its core size
#    and leaves max at Integer.MAX_VALUE) — read `executor_pool_core_threads`, which is 200; and
#    `executor_queued_tasks` includes retries still in backoff.
#
# ALL FOUR NOW SHARE ONE FLAT POOL of DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=350, and all four fit it with headroom:
#
#   TO-1  99 http + 200 worker + 10 outbox poller  + 1 republisher = 310
#   TO-2  99 http + 200 worker + 20 notify workers + 1 republisher = 320
#   TO-3  99 http + 200 worker +                     1 republisher = 300
#   TO-4  99 http + 200 worker +                     1 republisher = 300
#
# The worker term is 200 because the retry lane merged into it (see 3 above); the sums are the same
# as when it was 150 worker + 50 retry, which is what makes runs across the merge comparable. Both
# compose and every branch's application.yaml default that 200, so an unset run gets it.
#
# One connection per demander — every TO handler is Propagation.REQUIRED, where a busy ES thread
# holds two. The pool was 300 until 2026-08-17, which left TO-1 and TO-2 oversubscribed; nothing
# warns when a flat pool runs short, so the symptom is Hikari wait time.
#
# TO-1 differs from the others in where @Async work runs, and it is PRE-EXISTING rather than a
# consequence of the port: it has TWO TaskExecutor beans (orderWorkerExecutor + outboxPollerExecutor),
# so @Async cannot resolve a unique one, finds no bean named `taskExecutor`, and falls back to
# SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor — a new thread per invocation. On TO-2/TO-3/TO-4 the same
# @ApplicationModuleListener runs on the order-worker pool, where event DELIVERY competes with
# reservation EXECUTION. Do not read a TO-1 vs TO-2/TO-4 delivery difference as a design property
# without accounting for it.
#
# The knobs below are forwarded by docker-compose. They NO LONGER MATCH TO-3's shipped values —
# ORDER_WORKER_THREADS deliberately diverges (50 here, 200 in every branch's application.yaml), and
# a compose default always sets the variable and therefore OVERRIDES the branch. That override is
# now the mechanism rather than an accident: it is the only way to give the whole family one width,
# since a branch cannot express a width the harness will honour.
#   ORDER_WORKER_THREADS  (50) — the whole TO family, merged-pool and blocking-@Retryable alike.
#       A blocking backoff rarely starves a pool this wide, so a retry-mechanism A/B is expected to
#       show little at 200 and to diverge as it falls (64, 32, 16). A null result at 200 is the
#       predicted result, not a failed run.
#   ORDER_RETRY_THREADS   (50) — equal to ORDER_WORKER_THREADS gives "separate lane, equally wide";
#       lower reproduces a narrow waist.
#   ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL (true) — above.
#   HTTP_THREADS (99) — see the pool section.
#   EVENT_DELIVERY_THREADS (100) — the drain's delivery pool on TO-2, TO-2-fix-A and TO-2-push,
#       raised from the 20 all three ship. Inert on TO-3/TO-4, which have no drain pool (their
#       @ApplicationModuleListener deliveries run on SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor, one unbounded thread
#       per task), and on TO-1, whose pool answers to the next name instead.
#   OUTBOX_POLLER_THREADS (100) — TO-1's delivery pool, raised from the 10 it ships. Separate name
#       because TO-1 polls rather than listens, so its workers live under app.outbox-poller. It was
#       absent from docker-compose entirely until 2026-08-25, so no archived TO-1 run was taken at
#       anything but 10 — the pre/post comparison on TO-1 is a 10x delivery-width change, not a
#       drain-topology result.
#
#   CONNECTION BUDGET after the 2026-08-25 change: the flat Hikari pool is DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=350
#   and Postgres allows 600. Peak demand is roughly http(99) + workers(50) + delivery(100) ≈ 250 on
#   every TO branch, against 200+20+99 ≈ 320 before, so the change LOWERS connection pressure even
#   though two of the three pools grew.
#
# THE POOL SPLIT, AND WHY IT IS GONE (removed 2026-08-17). TO-3 briefly ran two Hikari pools behind
# a lane-routed DataSource — `app-pool` for HTTP reads, `write-pool` for the accept transaction,
# order workers, retry pool and republisher — mirroring ES's spring.datasource + axonDataSource
# split. The argument for it stands and is worth restating, because it is the argument for reading
# TO's pool numbers carefully rather than for the code: ES's write path CANNOT starve ES's HTTP
# reads, TO's can. That is the mechanism behind the TO-2 collapse (Hikari pegged at 300 active /
# ~119 pending, the accept path sharing the pool, throughput halved), and comparing the families on
# pool pressure measures that topology difference rather than a persistence model.
#
# It was removed anyway, and two facts made the isolation cheap to give up. First, k6's read
# scenario is gated on READ_RATE, which defaults to 0 — so on a standard run NOTHING calls the three
# GET endpoints and app-pool sat idle, holding 50 connections open (Hikari's minimumIdle defaults to
# maximumPoolSize) to protect traffic that was not there. Second, keeping it meant TO-3 alone had a
# DataSource topology no other TO branch had, on top of the retry rebuild it already carried.
#
# What replaced it: ONE flat pool on every TO branch, DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=350 from .env, covering
# http + worker + retry + delivery + republisher with headroom on all four. Nothing recomputes that
# sum at startup any more and nothing logs `[POOLS]`, so an over-wide sweep shows up as Hikari wait
# time rather than a warning — raising HTTP_THREADS or ORDER_WORKER_THREADS is still a pool change
# even though it does not look like one.
#
# If the isolation is ever wanted back, git history has it: the branch carried it from 2026-08-15
# (5a163df) to 2026-08-17, and the read side only means something under a run with READ_RATE set.
#
# Unlike ES, TO holds ONE connection per busy thread: every handler is Propagation.REQUIRED, so
# there is no second, non-transactional connection like the one Axon's DataSourceConnectionProvider
# takes per event-store call. That 2:1 is a property of the persistence model, not a setting.
#
# THE ENVELOPES (2026-08-17). Both families SPEND their write pool: each one's write-lane threads
# are sized so their peak demand equals the pool that serves them. Because TO holds one connection
# per busy thread and ES two, they buy
#
#   TO-3              300 demanders:  99 http + 150 worker + 50 retry + 1 republisher
#                     in one flat pool of 350 per node
#   ES-4              175 write-lane threads: 112 command + 30 retry-as-timer + 60 saga
#                                              + 3 projections
#                                              (+ 99 http, NOT in the sum — see its entry)
#                     + 50 hikari = 400 per node
#
# That ~2:1 connection cost per thread is a property of the persistence model — a RESULT, not a
# setting. ES-1 and ES-2 carry the SAME shape as of 2026-08-20 — same 112/30/60/3, same 350, same
# 99 Tomcat threads outside the sum — so any of the three serves as the ES side of a cross-family
# table and the three differ only in the persistence design they exist to compare — which is what
# the registry is for. (The retired ES-3 kept the two-lane 82 + 30 shape; that and its pessimistic
# lock are why it is no longer a row. This table named ES-4-NullLock-A until 2026-08-17, with a
# `12 http + 91 command` shape that branch never actually implemented.)
#
# THE TWO ENVELOPES NO LONGER MATCH: 350 against 400, where 2026-08-16 had them equal at 400. TO's
# write pool went 350 -> 300 with its worker lane (200 -> 150) and ES was deliberately left alone.
# So the families present Postgres with different footprints again — the very confound the equal
# envelope was introduced to remove. It is accepted, not overlooked; any cross-family table built
# from runs after this date must say so, and runs from before it are not config-identical to runs
# after, so do not mix them in one table.
#
# THE LANE WIDTHS ARE NOT A CROSS-FAMILY RATIO. An earlier version mirrored TO's admission : worker
# : retry shape onto ES; that was wrong, because there is no thread-for-thread correspondence
# between the families. TO carries a whole order on ONE thread for ~6-11 ms. On ES an order costs N+2 saga
# activations at ~6.7 ms and N+1 command executions at ~3 ms, so by Little's law the saga lane needs
# ~2.4x the command lane — where the mirrored shape gave it a fifth.
#
# Measured on ES-4-NullLock-A_capacity_W-base_20260815T212646Z: saga pool 12/12 active with 48
# queued for the entire 21-minute load phase, command pool ~7 busy of 91, axon-jdbc-pool 30-39
# active of 350, order e2e p95 111 s. The saga lane was the constraint and 90% of the connection
# budget went unused. SAGA_WORKER_THREADS therefore goes 12 -> 64, total-segments 60 -> 64 to match
# (a segment is processed sequentially, so it is the concurrency ceiling), and Tomcat takes the
# remainder at 12 — the accept path is one aggregate append against an order costing N+1 of them.
#
# WriteLaneCoverageTest and AxonPoolCoverageTest each assert their own half of the budget.
#
# Read `hikaricp_connections_active` per pool, plus `order_retry_pool_active` (TO) and
# `saga_pool_active{pool="retry"}` (ES): a run that pinned either retry pool at its ceiling measured
# pool width, not the persistence model, and should be re-run wider. Both families also leave
# Tomcat's accept-count at Boot's default 100, so offered load beyond the HTTP thread cap is
# REFUSED past that backlog — check the 5xx rate before reading a run as merely slow.
#
# `ES-4` IS THE LOCK-FREE BRANCH AS OF 2026-08-20. The aggregate lock is GONE — `NullLockFactory`
# instead of `PessimisticLockFactory` on the InventoryItem repository — so concurrent commands on
# one aggregate race for the same sequence number and are resolved by the event store's unique
# constraint plus the gateway retry instead of by in-JVM serialisation. The copy-on-write cache, the
# cache-fed snapshotter and the saga are unchanged.
#
# This arrived by adopting `ES-4-NullLock`'s tree wholesale (see the ADOPTION note below), so the
# branch also carries: Tomcat capped at 99 (`HTTP_THREADS`, matching TO-3's admission width), the
# command pool at 112, the retry pool demoted to a pure timer, and `axon.eventstore.max-gap-offset`
# at 500 instead of Axon's 10000 — load-bearing once rolled-back appends are routine rather than
# rare, which is exactly what a lock-free branch makes them.
#
# 112 is not a width chosen on its own merits. It is the residue of the connection budget once the
# saga's 60 and the 3 projections are paid for, PLUS the 30 the retry lane no longer executes on:
# a thread that only calls execute(...) opens no transaction and takes no connection, so the retry
# lane drops out of the sum entirely. The branch consumes the 350 that docker-compose passes
# EXACTLY: 2 x (112 + 0 + 60 + 3) = 350, and since 2026-08-20 ES-1 and ES-2 do too. The two-lane
# shape they all ran until then spent the same 350 as 2 x (82 + 30 + 60 + 3), which is what makes
# runs from either side of the port comparable.
#
# **THE PESSIMISTIC-LOCK CODE IS NOT IN THE SUITE ANY MORE**, on any ES design point. It is still
# REACHABLE — the adoption commit's first parent is the old ES-4 tip (1dead38) — but nothing in this
# registry builds it. Runs filed under `ES-4` from 2026-08-20 measure lock-free code; older `ES-4`
# runs measure `PessimisticLockFactory`. THEY DO NOT BELONG IN THE SAME TABLE and nothing in the
# harness can tell them apart, because the variant name is identical. Date the run.
#
# The 99 Tomcat threads are deliberately OUTSIDE that budget and are a known gap, not an oversight:
# createOrderReservation calls sendAndWait on the Tomcat thread and SimpleCommandBus handles it
# there, so an in-flight POST holds two axon-jdbc-pool connections like any command thread. True
# peak demand is 2 x (99 + 175) = 548 against a pool of 350; it holds only because offered load,
# not the thread cap, bounds POSTs in flight. Capping Tomcat low and deriving the pool from the full
# sum would close it; NO BRANCH ACTUALLY DOES THIS — the ES-4-NullLock-A notes claim it, but that
# branch has no `server.tomcat` block in code. Watch
# `hikaricp_connections_timeout_total{pool="axon-jdbc-pool"}` — a starved command stalls 5s and
# then fails TERMINALLY, showing up as latency and rejections rather than as an error.
#
# THE RETRY POOL ON ES-4 IS A TIMER, NOT A LANE. This was `ES-4-NullLock-oneExec`, a registry row of
# its own until 2026-08-20; it merged into ES-4-NullLock that day (099a694) and came here with the
# adoption, so the row is RETIRED and the A/B is against runs, not against a sibling variant. The
# retried command is handed back to `sagaCommandExecutor` instead of executing on the retry pool,
# and that pool absorbs the 30 threads the retry lane no longer executes on (command 82 -> 112). It
# is the ES analogue of TO's ORDER_RETRY_EXECUTE_ON_RETRY_POOL=false.
#
# **ES-1 AND ES-2 TOOK IT ON 2026-08-20** (ES-1 2204e15, ES-2 40701a3 — the same change twice, the
# two files having been byte-identical). All three lock-free design points now run one pool, so an
# ES-1-vs-ES-4 difference is the persistence design and not the retry topology. Expect
# `saga_pool_active{pool="retry"}` near 0 on all three. (The retired ES-3 kept the two-lane shape
# and cannot leave it without also leaving the pessimistic lock — one of the two reasons its row
# went; see docs/retired-variants.md.)
#
# Each port is verified by `RetryDispatchTargetTest` (the hand-off, and the 350 sum) and
# `PoolVisibilityTest` (thread names and the six gauges), which came across with the code: 35 tests
# green on ES-1, 36 on ES-2.
#
# It exists because the two-lane shape was not a design choice on the other ES branches — it is a
# consequence nobody opted into. Axon's `RetryingCallback.RetryDispatch.run()` calls
# `commandBus.dispatch()` INLINE and the autoconfigured SimpleCommandBus handles on the calling
# thread, so the whole retried command executes wherever the scheduled task runs. Axon documents
# neither that consequence nor any sizing guidance for the retry executor; it follows from two
# documented properties (SimpleCommandBus runs on the dispatching thread; RetryScheduler schedules
# a task on its executor) plus verification against the 4.11.2 bytecode. Treat it as version-
# specific behaviour rather than a guarantee.
#
# THE CHANGE WAS SINGLE-VARIABLE BY CONSTRUCTION, which is rare here and the reason to trust the
# archived pair:
#
#   before (ES-4-NullLock)  2 x ( 82 command + 30 retry + 60 saga + 3 projections) = 350
#   after  (ES-4 today)     2 x (112 command +  0 retry + 60 saga + 3 projections) = 350
#
# Equal executing threads (175), equal Axon connections (350, the AXON_JDBC_POOL_SIZE default),
# identical retry policy (5 attempts, 25/50/100/200 ms), identical Tomcat cap (99, outside both
# sums). The retry lane drops out because a thread that only calls execute(...) opens no
# transaction and takes no connection, and the backoff is served in the DelayedWorkQueue on no
# thread at all. The archived pair is `bench-results/ES-4-NullLock_*` against
# `bench-results/ES-4-NullLock-oneExec_*` at DISTINCT_ITEMS=1 — the difference only exists on
# contended work, and a null result at low contention is the predicted result, not a failed run.
# On ES-1 and ES-2 the same comparison is against their own pre-2026-08-20 runs, there being no
# sibling variant. The retired ES-3 is NOT a stand-in for the two-lane half: it differs in the lock
# as well, so a comparison against it varies two things.
#
# Two effects push opposite ways, which is the question:
#   - QUEUE POSITION. A retry now rejoins an unbounded FIFO at the TAIL, behind first attempts
#     admitted after it, so its real wait becomes backoff + queue depth. This can raise
#     `inventory_optimistic_exhausted_total` even though the lane is wider.
#   - DISPOSITION CONTENTION. OrderReservationSaga submits abandon()/release/fail-order to the same
#     executor, so a saturated pool now delays terminal dispositions behind retried work. Watch
#     `saga_command_failed_total{stage="abandon-rejected"}`, which exists for exactly that.
#
# WHAT TO READ. Both pools are instrumented with the same names the origin-only `ES-4-NullLock-mod`
# uses, so panels built for that branch resolve here:
# `saga_pool_{active,queued,size}{pool="command"|"retry"}`.
# The two lanes mean different things here than they did under the two-lane shape:
#   pool="command"  active/queued is the whole story — `queued` is where a retry waits BEHIND newer
#                   first attempts, which is the cost this branch trades its wider lane for.
#   pool="retry"    `active` should sit at ~0; these threads only hand tasks over. Sustained
#                   activity there means work is executing on the timer, which it must not.
#                   `queued` is the in-flight retry count (a ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor holds
#                   delayed tasks in its DelayedWorkQueue), NOT threads waiting for a slot.
# Note `size` is threads ALIVE, not the configured width: the pools fill lazily, so the series ramps
# and a mid-run value below 112 means only that many threads were ever needed at once.
#
# Threads are NAMED (`saga-command-N`, `retry-timer-N`) where every other ES branch leaves Executors'
# default `pool-N-thread-M`. That is what makes `jcmd <pid> Thread.print` answer "where does a retry
# execute?" by inspection, and logback's `[%thread]` pattern carries it into every line. Setting
# `logging.level.pl.szymanski.wiktor.config.ConcurrencyRetryScheduler=DEBUG` prints
# `[RETRY] executing on saga-command-N` per retry — the branch's headline property as one grep.
#
# It publishes one series no other branch has, `inventory_retry_handoff_rejected_total`. It should
# be 0: it counts retries that ran on the retry thread after the command pool refused the hand-off
# (shutdown only — the queue is unbounded), i.e. work that escaped the budgeted lane.
#
# THE ADOPTION (2026-08-20). `ES-1`, `ES-2` and `ES-4` now CARRY the lock-free code that used to
# live on `ES-1-NullLock`, `ES-2-NullLock` and `ES-4-NullLock`. The pessimistic branches were the
# obsolete ones, so the suffixed branches were folded into the plain names rather than replacing
# them in the registry, and the `*-NullLock` rows are gone.
#
# HOW, so it can be undone. Each of the three got ONE merge commit whose tree is byte-identical to
# the `-NullLock` tip, with the old pessimistic tip as first parent and the `-NullLock` tip as
# second:
#
#   ES-1  90b164a  tree of cb72672   old tip ec63eca   (first parent)
#   ES-2  e68f790  tree of cfb81ef   old tip 0603497
#   ES-4  9899417  tree of 604b560   old tip 1dead38
#
# `git diff ES-1 <old tip>` still shows exactly what the lock cost in code. The three local
# `*-NullLock` branches were DELETED after the merge (they were byte-identical refs and would have
# benched the same binary twice); origin still has all three, and their commits are reachable from
# the plain branches regardless.
#
# **THE HAZARD THIS CREATES, and nothing in the harness can catch it:** a run directory named
# `ES-1_*`, `ES-2_*` or `ES-4_*` means DIFFERENT CODE depending on when it was made. Before
# 2026-08-20 it is `PessimisticLockFactory` with Boot's default 200 Tomcat threads; after, it is
# `NullLockFactory` at the widths below. The variant name is the only identity the harness records,
# and it did not change. Date every archived ES run before it enters a table. Archived
# `ES-{1,2,4}-NullLock_*` directories are all lock-free and all PRE-oneExec — 82 command + 30
# EXECUTING retry, where all three branches are now 112 + 0. They are unambiguous, and they are the
# two-lane half of the retry-topology comparison, not a rerun of today's code.
#
# WHAT THE LOCK-FREE SIDE ACTUALLY CHANGED, all of it now baseline. Not one thing, THREE — worth
# keeping straight because it means no in-tree pair isolates the LockFactory any more:
#   1. the repository. ES-1/ES-2 declared no repository bean at all and let Axon's implicit
#      @Aggregate registration lock pessimistically, so the lock-free repository had to be
#      introduced AND named on `@Aggregate(repository = ...)`.
#   2. `axon.eventstore.max-gap-offset` 500 instead of Axon's 10000, load-bearing once rolled-back
#      appends are routine rather than rare — which is what removing the lock makes them.
#   3. the lane widths: command pool 64 -> 82, retry 23 -> 30, Tomcat 200 -> 99. All three went
#      further still on 2026-08-20, to 112 command + 0 executing retry (see the ES-4 entry).
#
# THE LOCK'S COST IS NO LONGER MEASURABLE IN THE SUITE. Restoring it means branching from a plain
# tip, putting `PessimisticLockFactory` back and changing NOTHING else, so that only the LockFactory
# differs — the old pessimistic tips will not serve, since they differ in all three respects above.
#
# `ES-3` was untouched by the adoption — there is no lock-free ES-3 and there cannot be one, which
# is why it stopped at three branches (below) — and its row was retired on 2026-08-20.
#
# ES-1, ES-2 and ES-4 all consume the 350 that docker-compose passes EXACTLY, one busy thread
# holding two Axon connections — 2 x (112 command + 0 retry + 60 saga + 3 projections) on all three
# since the retry port of 2026-08-20; ES-3 spends the same 350 as 2 x (82 + 30 + 60 + 3). Their 99 Tomcat threads sit OUTSIDE that sum by design — the
# accept command is dispatched with sendAndWait on the Tomcat thread and SimpleCommandBus handles it
# there, so peak demand is really 2 x (99 + 175) = 548 and the pool holds only because offered load,
# not the thread cap, bounds POSTs in flight. Watch
# `hikaricp_connections_timeout_total{pool="axon-jdbc-pool"}` on every run of the three: a starved
# command stalls 5s and then fails TERMINALLY, surfacing as latency and rejections, not as an error.
#
# THERE IS NO LOCK-FREE ES-3, and this is why the adoption stopped at three branches. ES-3 caches
# with Axon's `WeakReferenceCache` and has no copy-on-write, so without a lock concurrent commands
# would mutate one shared aggregate root. Only ES-4's repository deep-copies per load, which is what
# makes ES-4 safe without one. ES-3 therefore still runs `PessimisticLockFactory`, and since it also
# kept the two-lane retry shape it could only ever be a three-variable column — so its row was
# retired the same day. The branch is untouched; see docs/retired-variants.md.
#
# THE AXON_JDBC_POOL_SIZE DEFAULT IS GLOBAL TO THE ES FAMILY, and that is the cost of it:
# docker-compose has passed 350 since 2026-08-16 and it OVERRIDES whatever a branch's own
# application.yaml says. Every ES branch except the -mod and -retry sidecars — the registered ES-1,
# ES-2 and ES-4, the retired ES-3, and the pessimistic tips they superseded — asks for 300 in its
# own yaml and now gets 350, 50 connections of headroom they did not have. Their documented budgets still fit, so this is not a correctness change, but
# runs made before and after 2026-08-16 are not config-identical and should not share a table.
# -mod (500) and -retry still need their own larger exports; the default does not cover them.
#
# One harness caveat, and it belongs here because the suite cannot see it: evaluate.py's
# `saga_command_failed_single_node` VALIDITY check assumes a JVM-local lock makes a 23505
# impossible at REPLICAS=1. Since the adoption that is false for ES-1, ES-2 and ES-4 by
# construction, so a single-node run of any of the three reports INVALID whenever any command
# exhausts its retries. Read the rest of the check list; the run is sound if that is its only
# failure. (The retired ES-3 was the last ES variant the assumption held for, so no registered
# variant satisfies it now.)
#
# Order matters: run-suite.sh runs them top to bottom. Families are grouped so a single
# suite pass crosses the TO/ES boundary once rather than once per variant; each crossing
# costs a full stack teardown because the two families use different compose service names.

TO-1  TO-1  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-2  TO-2  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-2-fix-A  TO-2-fix-A  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-2-push  TO-2-push  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-3  TO-3  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
TO-4  TO-4  TO  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-1  ES-1  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-2  ES-2  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
ES-4  ES-4  ES  reserve-delay,payload-bytes
